WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.255
Lesson 4.7.

00:00:02.255 --> 00:00:06.230
Stablecoin savings rates and yield-bearing stablecoins.

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:08.607
By the end of this lesson, you'll be able

00:00:08.607 --> 00:00:11.437
to tell apart the different ways a stablecoin can

00:00:11.437 --> 00:00:13.475
pay yield, and the risk behind each.

00:00:14.775 --> 00:00:16.065
Here's the plan.

00:00:16.065 --> 00:00:17.750
First, the key ideas.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:20.465
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:20.465 --> 00:00:21.990
Then, your checklist.

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.152
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:25.452 --> 00:00:29.101
Yield on "dollars" on-chain comes from one of four

00:00:29.101 --> 00:00:34.051
places: Same "4–10% on dollars", four completely different risks.

00:00:34.051 --> 00:00:38.396
First, Lending: you lend stablecoins to borrowers (Module 3).

00:00:38.396 --> 00:00:41.390
Risk: the protocol, collateral and oracles.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:44.989
Next, Savings rates: some stablecoin protocols pay a

00:00:44.989 --> 00:00:47.637
set rate to holders who deposit into a

00:00:47.637 --> 00:00:51.060
savings contract, funded by their own revenue.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:54.915
Risk: the stablecoin's design and governance setting the rate.

00:00:54.915 --> 00:00:59.614
Then, Treasury-backed: the issuer holds short-term government debt and

00:00:59.614 --> 00:01:02.826
passes on part of the interest (Lesson 4.8).

00:01:02.826 --> 00:01:06.326
Risk: the issuer, custodian and redemption terms.

00:01:06.326 --> 00:01:09.717
And finally, Synthetic / basis-backed: yield comes

00:01:09.717 --> 00:01:12.776
from a hedged derivatives position (spot long

00:01:12.776 --> 00:01:15.071
+ perp short) earning funding.

00:01:15.071 --> 00:01:18.477
Risk: funding turns negative, exchange failure,

00:01:18.477 --> 00:01:21.031
and the design itself (Lesson 10.3).

00:01:22.331 --> 00:01:24.846
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:24.846 --> 00:01:30.279
$10,000 in a yield-bearing stablecoin paying 4.5%

00:01:30.279 --> 00:01:34.165
earns ~$450/yr if the rate holds.

00:01:34.165 --> 00:01:37.217
Before buying, answer: where does the

00:01:37.217 --> 00:01:39.805
4.5% come from (1–4 above)?

00:01:39.805 --> 00:01:41.095
What happens to it in a

00:01:41.095 --> 00:01:43.610
bear market when funding goes negative?

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:46.010
Can you redeem for $1, and who can?

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:48.770
Here's your checklist.

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:50.305
Do it now, for real.

00:01:50.305 --> 00:01:53.032
First, Yield source identified (lending,

00:01:53.032 --> 00:01:55.660
savings rate, treasuries or basis).

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:58.840
Next, Redemption path and who can redeem known.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.188
And finally, Split across different yield

00:02:01.188 --> 00:02:03.200
sources, not just different tickers.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:05.710
Question 1.

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:08.160
Name the four sources of stablecoin yield.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:15.574
The answer: Lending, protocol savings rates,

00:02:15.574 --> 00:02:19.450
treasury backing, and synthetic basis/funding positions.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:21.995
Question 2.

00:02:21.995 --> 00:02:25.140
Why can a basis-backed stablecoin's yield fall to zero?

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:32.780
The answer: Funding can turn negative in bear markets.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:35.350
Question 3.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:40.075
Two yield-bearing stablecoins both backed by basis trades: diversified?

00:02:44.495 --> 00:02:46.200
The answer: Not really.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.835
They share the same yield source and failure mode.

00:02:50.135 --> 00:02:51.380
Let's recap.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:55.235
Lending: you lend stablecoins to borrowers (Module 3).

00:02:55.235 --> 00:02:58.647
Savings rates: some stablecoin protocols pay a set

00:02:58.647 --> 00:03:01.523
rate to holders who deposit into a savings

00:03:01.523 --> 00:03:04.365
contract, funded by their own revenue.

00:03:04.365 --> 00:03:08.581
Treasury-backed: the issuer holds short-term government debt and

00:03:08.581 --> 00:03:11.735
passes on part of the interest (Lesson 4.8).

00:03:11.735 --> 00:03:16.019
Synthetic / basis-backed: yield comes from a hedged derivatives

00:03:16.019 --> 00:03:19.365
position (spot long + perp short) earning funding.

00:03:20.665 --> 00:03:23.180
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:25.922
Next up, Lesson 4.8: Tokenized

00:03:25.922 --> 00:03:29.460
treasuries and real-world assets (RWAs).
